{"text": "Lesson three, auto-regression and the KIVI cache. A decoder-only language model factorizes a sequence with the chain rule: each next token is conditioned on everything before it. At each layer, the hidden states are projected into queries, keys, and values. Causal softmax attention mixes them. During decoding, only one new query is formed, and it attends over every past key. Recomputing keys and values from scratch every step would be quadratic. The engine instead depends the new key and the new value and reuses history. Per-step compute drops from quadratic in length to linear. Memory becomes linear in length. That stored history is the KIVI cache. The weights do not grow. The cache does. The footprint formula is the one to manipulate cold. Cache bytes equal two times number of layers times number of key value heads times head dimension times sequence length times batch size times bytes per element. The leading two Keys versus values, stored as separate tensors. Bytes per element is two for 16-bit floats, one for 8-bit, and one half for 4-bit. Each pillar pulls a different term. Grouped query attention and multi-query attention reduce the number of key value heads. Quantization reduces bytes per element. Eviction and sliding windows reduce the effective length. A bigger batch multiplies the cache. That buys throughput and spends capacity. Multi-head latent attention does not fit this formula. Used in DeepSeek, it stores one low-rank latent vector per token and reconstructs keys and values at attention time. The leading two disappears. The survey keeps the formula as the baseline and treats latent attention as a structural change. Paging does not shrink the formula either. It stops you from reserving the maximum length up front and fragmenting the rest of memory. Prefill writes the cache for the prompt in one compute-bound pass. Decode reads the whole history, attends, and appends one Production blurs the textbook split. Chunked prefill slices a long prompt so decode can be interleaved. Continuous batching admits new requests at every step instead of waiting for a whole batch to finish. Both still use this same cache.", "language": "en", "duration": 134.448, "words": [{"text": "Lesson", "start": 0.14, "end": 0.44}, {"text": "three,", "start": 0.54, "end": 0.82}, {"text": "auto-regression", "start": 1.42, "end": 2.201}, {"text": "and", "start": 2.321, "end": 2.421}, {"text": "the", "start": 2.421, "end": 2.501}, {"text": "KIVI", "start": 2.561, "end": 2.921}, {"text": "cache.", "start": 3.241, "end": 3.681}, {"text": "A", "start": 4.221, "end": 4.241}, {"text": "decoder-only", "start": 4.301, "end": 5.042}, {"text": "language", "start": 5.122, "end": 5.522}, {"text": "model", "start": 5.562, "end": 5.822}, {"text": "factorizes", "start": 5.922, "end": 6.642}, {"text": "a", "start": 6.682, "end": 6.702}, {"text": "sequence", "start": 6.802, "end": 7.262}, {"text": "with", "start": 7.282, "end": 7.402}, {"text": "the", "start": 7.422, "end": 7.482}, {"text": "chain", "start": 7.543, "end": 7.783}, {"text": "rule:", "start": 7.863, "end": 8.103}, {"text": "each", "start": 8.623, "end": 8.803}, {"text": "next", "start": 8.863, "end": 9.103}, {"text": "token", "start": 9.143, "end": 9.463}, {"text": "is", "start": 9.523, "end": 9.603}, {"text": "conditioned", "start": 9.643, "end": 10.143}, {"text": "on", "start": 10.183, "end": 10.263}, {"text": "everything", "start": 10.423, "end": 10.844}, {"text": "before", "start": 10.884, "end": 11.224}, {"text": "it.", "start": 11.264, "end": 11.364}, {"text": "At", "start": 11.864, "end": 11.964}, {"text": "each", "start": 12.024, "end": 12.204}, {"text": "layer,", "start": 12.284, "end": 12.584}, {"text": "the", "start": 12.924, "end": 12.984}, {"text": "hidden", "start": 13.024, "end": 13.244}, {"text": "states", "start": 13.324, "end": 13.625}, {"text": "are", "start": 13.645, "end": 13.745}, {"text": "projected", "start": 13.745, "end": 14.225}, {"text": "into", "start": 14.265